  "query_preview": "..\n\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Strong to severe thunderstorms this afternoon and evening,\n  bringing the threat of strong winds, large hail, and heavy rain.\n\n- Elevated fire weather this weekend from hot, dry, and breezy \n  conditions, especially in northwest Montana.\n\nHigh resolution models show convection forming over the southern \nBitterroot mountains around 2 to 3pm, and will move northeastward\ninto west-central and southwest Montana. Gusty winds will be the \nmain concern with these storms, with gusts 40 to 50 mph \nanticipated with stronger cells. While low (10 to 15 percent),\nthere is a chance thunderstorms could form as far north as\nnorthwest Montana this evening.\n\nLike yesterday, storms will also be capable of very heavy rainfall\nwith rates in excess of 0.50 inches per hour. Sensitive flood-\nprone areas and recent burn scars, particularly in Lemhi County \nand southwest Montana, could have flood concerns if they take a \ndirect hit from a thunderstorms.\n\ntoday, the low pressure system that has been sitting off the \nPacific Northwest coast slowly moves through southern British \nColumbia and Alberta, flattening the ridge of high pressure and \nbringing more westerly flow aloft. This will shift thunderstorm \npotential primarily to locations south of Interstate 90. Further \nnorth across northwest Montana, breezy westerly winds will \ndevelop. This coupled with much drier air could enhance fire \nweather concerns. By Sunday, much drier air moves over all of \nwestern Montana and north central Idaho, with hot, dry and breezy \nconditions anticipated.\n\nLong-range weather models suggest the next surge of monsoon\nmoisture will arrive by the middle of next week. chances for\nshowers and thunderstorm will trend higher each day as moisture\nlevels reach over one inch.",
